  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM EDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nA short wave trough continues to dive southeast across southeast \nOntario this morning. Low level ridging is being reinforced behind \nthis short wave across the Mid MS River Valley, and this will \nprolong the period of weak gradient that has been in place locally \nthe past several days. Ideal radiational cooling conditions this \nmorning will continue to support some patchy fog potential, and \npresent indications still suggest that southeast portions of the \narea downwind of eastern Great Lakes may experience a brief period \nof greater fog coverage this morning south of US Route 24 across \nnortheast Indiana and northwest Ohio. Otherwise for today large \nscale flow amplification is expected as a stronger upper vort lobe \nacross southeast Canada phases with an Atlantic vort max. Meanwhile, \na cut-off upper low will continue to migrate slowly eastward into \nsouthern California this evening. This evolution will result an \namplifying low/upper level ridging across central CONUS and south \ncentral Canada. Low level/near sfc winds will veer more easterly as \nthis amplification occurs which will result in continued weak \nthermal advections over the next 24 hours. Thus, a persistence \nforecast has been utilized once again today in terms of max \ntemps. The veering low level flow may allow some slightly better\nlow level moisture to drop south into the area from the Great \nLakes, but the only possible effect from this weak advection \nwould be some very low coverage diurnal cu that will not have \nany impact on temps today.\n\nThis transition back to low level easterlies will likely support \nadditional stratus/fog development downwind of the eastern Great \nLakes tonight, with a potential of some dense fog early Friday \nmorning across northwest Ohio, south central Michigan, and \npossibly into portions of northeast Indiana. Low temperatures \ntonight will drop back into the low 40s once again, but may dip \ninto upper 30s for favored, cool low-lying spots.\n\nGuidance is starting to come into a bit better agreement \nregarding large scale flow through the weekend. A stronger and \nmore progressive northern stream upper level system will shift \nacross south central Canada this weekend which will drive a cold\nfront boundary southward across the Great Lakes. Track of this \nnorthern stream wave is expected to remain well to the north, \nand departure of this wave will likely hang up the front north \nof the local area through the weekend. With forcing/frontal \nposition to the north, and narrow zone of better pre-frontal \nmoisture transport remaining from the Central Plains to the \nnorthern Great Lakes, a dry forecast remains intact through \nMonday. The warmest days of this forecast period still look to \nbe Sunday/Monday as veered low level flow allows for some modest\nincrease in low level warm advection.\n\nBy Tuesday/Wednesday the southern stream cut-off low is expected to \neject east and dampen. This wave could interact with the stalled \nfrontal boundary across the Upper MS Valley to the Great Lakes with \nsome weak isentropic ascent possible. Given dampening nature of this \nwave and likely marginal moisture profiles, blended PoPs remain only \nin the 10 to 20 percent range toward the end of this forecast valid \nperiod.",
